      "design": "r0A",
      "good": "The team names wrap at word boundaries, while headers, numbers and signed goal differences stay intact. Consistent two-line rows make each record easy to follow, and the compact statistical columns keep related measures close together.",
      "problems": "The initial viewport cuts through GD and leaves Pts offscreen, so readers must scroll to see two measures central to the standings. The table is relatively tall because every team name wraps.",
      "cause": "The 108px team column provides just enough room for the longest individual word but not for complete names. Together with the widths needed to keep the statistics intact, it makes the table 441px wide."
    },
    {
      "design": "r0B",
      "good": "More of the statistical columns appear in the initial viewport, and the table requires less horizontal scrolling than the other designs.",
      "problems": "Team names, headers, numbers and signed differences split across lines. Uneven, tall rows make it difficult to identify teams and compare records, and Pts is still partly offscreen.",
      "cause": "The 90px team column leaves only 69px for text, less than the longest team-name word. The 38px statistical columns leave 17px for text, which is too narrow for several values and headers."
    },
    {
      "design": "r0C",
      "good": "Every name and statistic stays on one line, producing compact, even rows and making each record easy to scan across.",
      "problems": "The wide team column pushes most goal statistics and points beyond the initial viewport. Readers must scroll substantially to compare the measures most relevant to standings.",
      "cause": "The 189px team column accommodates the longest full name, but contributes to a 522px table, 162px wider than the viewport."
    }
  ],
